# Next.js App Router Conventions

## Default to Server Components

Every component is a Server Component unless it needs the browser. Do not add `"use client"` by default.

## When to add `"use client"`

Only add `"use client"` when the component needs one of:
- Browser APIs (`window`, `document`, `navigator`)
- Event handlers (`onClick`, `onChange`, `onSubmit`)
- React hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, custom hooks)
- Third-party libs that require the browser (Tone.js, etc.)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — use client added without reason
"use client"
export function ChordTitle({ title }: { title: string }) {
    return <h1>{title}</h1>   // no browser APIs, no hooks
}

// ✅ GOOD — Server Component, no directive needed
export function ChordTitle({ title }: { title: string }) {
    return <h1>{title}</h1>
}
```

## Push `"use client"` to the leaves

Keep the `"use client"` boundary as deep in the tree as possible. A parent Server Component can render a Client Component child -- not the reverse.

```
app/chords/page.tsx         ← Server Component (no directive)
  └── ProgressionPanel      ← Server Component
        └── ChordPlayer     ← "use client" (needs Tone.js + hooks)
```

## No `async` in Client Components

`async/await` at the component level only works in Server Components. Data fetching in Client Components goes through hooks.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — async Client Component
"use client"
export async function ChordInput() { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — hook handles async in client
"use client"
export function ChordInput() {
    const { generate, isLoading } = useChordGenerator()
}
```

## Route handlers

API logic lives in `app/api/*/route.ts`. These are always Server-side -- never import them into client code.
